Interview with the             NannyMUD Times                         Page 4
ancient wizard: Vincent

Greetings Vincent, welcome to another NannyMUD Times interview...

  Q: Care you share your real name?  
  
  A: David Down

  Q: What's your players age?  

  A: 55

  Q: When did you start playing NannyMUD?  
  
  A: 1990

  Q: What guild did you start in?  
  
  A: It's faded from memory.

  Q: Was Vincent your first character?  
  
  A: Yes.

  Q: When did you become a wizard?  
  
  A: I think it was 92 or 93.

  Q: Why did you decide to become a wizard?  
  
  A: I enjoy programming and wanted to add some of my own things.

  Q: How did you become a high wizard?  
  
  A: I was invided by Brom after developing several innovative quests.

  Q: Why did you decide to retire from high wizhood?  
  
  A: There were some disagreements.

  Q: You've been gone for more than 6 years, why did you stop playing NannyMUD?  
  
  A: I started playing Asheron's Call, which is a 3D MMORPG.  
     I found that play there could be scripted with Jscript and the 
     3d environment was more interesting than a plain text MUD.

  Q: What have you been doing for 6 years? 

  A: I played AC for nearly 4 years and participated in many interesting 
     betas of other games. Two of the longer betas lasting a year or more 
     were Neocron and Earth and Beyond.  I enjoyed A Tale in the Desert 
     for about 7 months and Star Wars Galaxies for about 9 months. 
 
     Then I was invited to the World of Warcraft beta and have been playing 
     it for the last 2 years. It has only of the best scripting UIs of 
     any game. I wrote nearly a dozen addons for it, however, with the 
     release of WoW 2.0 there were some major restrictions added that 
     ruined many of my addons, so I quit playing just this month.
 
     I bought The Elder Scrolls IV: Oblivion and have been quite impressed 
     with the AI and graphics. I just wish it was multi-player. I've also 
     been trying some other betas and demos. I've also been playing 
     Civilization 4 by email with a friend. I'm currently looking for my 
     next online game.

  Q: Are you ever going to check back in?  
  
  A: I don't remember my password.

  Q: Aren't you curious if your area and quests are still open?  
  
  A: Yes, is it still a significant part of the game?

  Q: Tell us about the Hunters Guild.

  A: After developing my area and several quests, I thought it would be 
     interesting to create a guild.  I've always liked crafting, so I 
     designed it with crafted guild items.

  Q: Why did you decide to close the Hunters Guild?

  A: Brom decided that Hunters were too powerful and nerfed it.  After 
     that I lost interest in it.  This was also about the time I started 
     looking for another online game to play.

  Q: If you were me, what questions would you ask yourself?

  A: What would you like to do if you weren't limited by time and money?

  Q: What would you like to do if you weren't limited by time and money?

  A: I'd like to develop my own online game where a player starts stranded 
     in a foreign land and has to play 'Survivor' and develop their own 
     tools, weapons, etc.

  Q: Any final comments for our readers?

  A: I wonder how many people still play texted based games anymore.  I've 
     experimented a little with creating a 2D game using Python, but to 
     create anything useful would take a long time and much effort. 
     I'm suprised that NannyMUD even still exists in this day when most 
     games feature fancy graphics. 
     
     I might drop in just to see what's still there.

  Thank you Vincent for your time.
